WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:05.820
Hello everyone. This is Wednesday's lecture, lecture fifteen, on moments and moment-generating

00:05.920 --> 00:10.417
functions. It is Wackerly section three point nine. The video follows the

00:10.517 --> 00:14.007
slides on the course page, so keep them open if you like,

00:14.107 --> 00:17.219
and pause whenever you want to try a step yourself.

00:19.000 --> 00:23.708
Think back over chapter three. For every distribution we met, the mean and

00:23.808 --> 00:27.736
the variance cost us a new trick. For the binomial we used the

00:27.836 --> 00:33.064
binomial theorem. For the geometric, a geometric series. For the Poisson, we first

00:33.164 --> 00:36.767
had to compute the expected value of Y times Y minus one.

00:36.866 --> 00:39.855
Today we build one function per distribution that

00:39.955 --> 00:43.196
hands over every moment by differentiation. And, more

00:43.296 --> 00:46.222
important, that function names the distribution.

00:48.000 --> 00:52.639
Here is the question we started with. A bank's treasury in Baku counts

00:52.739 --> 00:57.582
large corporate payments, of a million manat or more, cleared per hour at

00:57.682 --> 01:01.781
two branches. At branch A the count is one or three, each with

01:01.881 --> 01:06.927
probability one half. At branch B it is two with probability three quarters,

01:07.027 --> 01:10.313
and zero or four with probability one eighth each.

01:10.400 --> 01:15.523
Both counts have mean two and variance one. Check that yourself; it takes

01:15.623 --> 01:19.673
a minute. So if two counts share a mean and a variance, is

01:19.773 --> 01:24.253
the risk the same? Clearly not. Branch B can produce a four, and

01:24.353 --> 01:28.690
branch A never can. Which extra numbers would tell them apart?

01:30.466 --> 01:35.238
Those numbers are the moments. Definition three point twelve: the k-th

01:35.338 --> 01:38.928
moment of Y about the origin is the expected value of

01:39.028 --> 01:41.364
Y to the k. We write it mu prime k.

01:41.433 --> 01:45.864
Definition three point thirteen: the k-th moment about the mean, the

01:45.964 --> 01:49.662
k-th central moment, is the expected value of Y minus mu,

01:49.762 --> 01:52.461
to the k. We write it mu k, with no prime.

01:52.533 --> 01:55.071
So mu prime one is the mean, and mu two is

01:55.171 --> 01:58.903
the variance. Theorem three point six, which you know as: the

01:59.003 --> 02:02.357
variance is E of Y squared minus mu squared, now reads:

02:02.457 --> 02:06.252
sigma squared equals mu prime two minus mu prime one, squared.

02:08.033 --> 02:12.658
Let's compute the first four moments for both branches, about the origin

02:12.758 --> 02:16.333
and about the mean, which is two for both. For branch A,

02:16.433 --> 02:20.467
Y to the fourth is one or eighty-one, each with probability one

02:20.567 --> 02:24.077
half, so mu prime four is forty-one. For branch B it is

02:24.177 --> 02:28.933
zero, sixteen or two hundred and fifty-six, with weights one eighth, three

02:29.033 --> 02:32.018
quarters and one eighth. That gives forty-four.

02:32.100 --> 02:36.558
The two branches agree on the first three moments and separate only

02:36.658 --> 02:40.437
at the fourth. The central fourth moment is one for A and

02:40.537 --> 02:44.111
four for B. Branch B puts more mass far from the mean:

02:44.211 --> 02:47.989
it has fatter tails. So mean and variance do not pin down

02:48.089 --> 02:52.140
a distribution. The whole sequence of moments, usually, does.

02:53.933 --> 02:58.128
To collect all the moments at once, we need one more definition.

02:58.228 --> 03:03.429
Definition three point fourteen: the moment-generating function, m of t, is the

03:03.529 --> 03:06.516
expected value of e to the t Y. We say the MGF

03:06.616 --> 03:10.207
exists if there is a positive constant b such that m of

03:10.307 --> 03:13.294
t is finite for every t between minus b and b.

03:13.366 --> 03:17.134
For a discrete Y it is a single sum: the sum over y of

03:17.234 --> 03:20.787
e to the t y times p of y. Notice two things. It is

03:20.887 --> 03:25.371
a function of t, not of y, because the y's have been summed out.

03:25.471 --> 03:29.811
And at t equals zero every exponential is one, so m of zero is

03:29.911 --> 03:35.183
the sum of the probabilities. Every MGF passes through the point zero, one.

03:35.266 --> 03:39.087
For our two branches, m A of t is one half e to the t,

03:39.187 --> 03:42.789
plus one half e to the three t. And m B of t is one

03:42.889 --> 03:47.726
eighth, plus three quarters e to the two t, plus one eighth e to the

03:47.826 --> 03:53.316
four t. Read it like this: the numbers multiplying t in the exponents are the

03:53.416 --> 03:58.180
values of Y, and the coefficients in front are their probabilities.

03:59.966 --> 04:04.605
Why is it called moment-generating? Expand e to the t y as

04:04.705 --> 04:08.854
a power series: one, plus t y, plus t y squared over

04:08.954 --> 04:13.920
two factorial, plus t y cubed over three factorial, and so on.

04:14.000 --> 04:19.067
Now take expectations term by term. The expected value of Y is mu prime

04:19.167 --> 04:23.724
one, the expected value of Y squared is mu prime two, and so on.

04:23.824 --> 04:27.436
So m of t is one, plus t times mu prime one, plus t

04:27.536 --> 04:31.584
squared over two factorial times mu prime two, and so on.

04:32.166 --> 04:35.819
The k-th moment is the coefficient of t to the k over k

04:35.919 --> 04:40.867
factorial. The MGF is every moment of Y packed into one function. Swapping

04:40.967 --> 04:45.848
the expectation with the infinite sum is justified whenever m of t exists

04:45.948 --> 04:49.464
near zero, which is why the definition asks for that.

04:51.233 --> 04:56.492
To get a moment back out, we differentiate. Theorem three point twelve:

04:56.592 --> 05:00.568
if m of t exists, then for any positive integer k, the

05:00.668 --> 05:04.116
k-th derivative of m at zero equals mu prime k.

05:04.200 --> 05:09.467
Watch it happen. Start from the series. Differentiate once: the constant

05:09.567 --> 05:13.568
one disappears, the t mu prime one term leaves mu prime

05:13.668 --> 05:17.519
one, and every other term still carries a power of t.

05:17.619 --> 05:20.948
Set t to zero, and only mu prime one survives.

05:21.033 --> 05:25.758
Differentiate twice, and only mu prime two survives at zero. In general,

05:25.858 --> 05:29.912
differentiating k times kills every term below t to the k, the

05:30.012 --> 05:33.330
t to the k term leaves mu prime k, and every higher

05:33.430 --> 05:36.345
term still has a t in it, so it dies at zero.

05:37.433 --> 05:41.146
In practice you need only two formulas. The mean is m prime of

05:41.246 --> 05:45.021
zero. And the variance is m double prime of zero, minus m prime

05:45.121 --> 05:50.002
of zero squared. That is theorem three point six again, written with derivatives.

05:51.800 --> 05:57.007
Now let's compute some MGFs. Example three point twenty-three. A motor insurer

05:57.107 --> 06:01.021
in Baku counts its claims per day, and the count is Poisson

06:01.121 --> 06:04.764
with mean lambda. By definition, m of t is the sum over

06:04.864 --> 06:07.962
y, from zero to infinity, of e to the t y times

06:08.062 --> 06:11.840
lambda to the y, e to the minus lambda, over y factorial.

06:11.933 --> 06:16.801
Take e to the minus lambda outside the sum. Then e to the t y times

06:16.901 --> 06:21.028
lambda to the y is lambda e to the t, all to the power y.

06:21.100 --> 06:27.076
What remains is the Taylor series of the exponential function, evaluated at lambda e

06:27.176 --> 06:30.693
to the t. So m of t is e to the minus lambda times

06:30.793 --> 06:34.816
e to the lambda e to the t. Combine the exponents: m of t

06:34.916 --> 06:38.867
equals e to the power lambda times e to the t minus one.

06:40.666 --> 06:45.469
Example three point twenty-four. Differentiate with the chain rule: m prime

06:45.569 --> 06:47.953
of t is e to the lambda times e to the

06:48.053 --> 06:51.679
t minus one, times lambda e to the t. Differentiate again

06:51.779 --> 06:54.620
with the product rule, and you get two terms.

06:54.700 --> 06:58.469
At t equals zero, every e to the something becomes one. So

06:58.569 --> 07:02.405
the mean is lambda, and mu prime two is lambda squared plus

07:02.505 --> 07:07.675
lambda. The variance is lambda squared plus lambda, minus lambda squared, which

07:07.775 --> 07:12.278
is lambda. That is theorem three point eleven, with no detour through

07:12.378 --> 07:15.080
the expected value of Y times Y minus one.

07:15.166 --> 07:17.985
With four point five claims a day, mu prime two is

07:18.085 --> 07:21.838
twenty-four point seven five, the variance is four point five, and

07:21.938 --> 07:24.758
the standard deviation is about two point one two.

07:26.533 --> 07:32.034
Next, the binomial. An FX dealer books ten independent trades a day, and each

07:32.134 --> 07:38.290
is profitable with probability point six. Y, the number of winning trades, is binomial

07:38.390 --> 07:41.999
with n ten and p point six. Its MGF is the sum of n

07:42.099 --> 07:45.636
choose y, times p e to the t, to the y, times q to

07:45.736 --> 07:49.855
the n minus y. By the binomial theorem, that is p e to the

07:49.955 --> 07:54.802
t plus q, all to the n. This is exercise three point one forty-five.

07:54.900 --> 08:00.333
Differentiate once. m prime of t is n times p e to the t plus q,

08:00.433 --> 08:05.261
to the n minus one, times p e to the t. At zero, p plus q

08:05.361 --> 08:10.708
is one, so m prime of zero is n p, which is six winning trades.

08:10.800 --> 08:15.379
Differentiate again, with the product rule. At zero, m double prime is

08:15.479 --> 08:18.789
n times n minus one times p squared, plus n p. That

08:18.889 --> 08:23.402
is ninety times point three six, which is thirty-two point four, plus

08:23.502 --> 08:28.617
six: thirty-eight point four. So the variance is thirty-eight point four minus

08:28.717 --> 08:32.160
six squared, which is two point four. And that is n p

08:32.260 --> 08:34.366
q, as we found in lecture eleven.

08:36.166 --> 08:42.404
Now suppose each winning trade earns two thousand manat and each losing trade costs

08:42.504 --> 08:47.596
one thousand. Then the daily profit and loss is X equals two Y minus

08:47.696 --> 08:51.949
ten minus Y, which is three Y minus ten. What is its MGF?

08:52.033 --> 08:57.310
Exercise three point one fifty-six gives the rule. If X is a Y plus b,

08:57.410 --> 09:01.996
then the expected value of e to the t times a Y plus b splits

09:02.096 --> 09:07.143
into e to the b t, which is a constant, times the expected value of

09:07.243 --> 09:11.061
e to the a t times Y. That is m Y evaluated at a t.

09:11.161 --> 09:15.286
The shift comes out as a factor; the scale goes inside.

09:15.366 --> 09:19.579
Here a is three and b is minus ten, so m X of t is e to the

09:19.679 --> 09:25.428
minus ten t, times point six e to the three t plus point four, all to the tenth.

09:25.500 --> 09:29.971
Differentiate with the product rule and set t to zero. You get b times

09:30.071 --> 09:33.367
m Y of zero plus a times m Y prime of zero, which is

09:33.467 --> 09:37.742
b plus a mu: three times six minus ten, eight thousand manat a day.

09:37.842 --> 09:42.640
Do the second derivative and the shift drops out of the variance, while the

09:42.740 --> 09:48.061
scale enters squared: nine times two point four, which is twenty-one point six. The

09:48.161 --> 09:52.175
standard deviation is about four point six five thousand manat.

09:53.966 --> 09:58.425
Here is a picture of theorem three point twelve. Take two counts with

09:58.525 --> 10:03.051
the same mean, four point five: a Poisson with lambda four point five,

10:03.151 --> 10:06.222
and a binomial with n ten and p point four five.

10:06.300 --> 10:09.900
Both curves pass through zero, one, as every MGF must. And

10:10.000 --> 10:13.792
both leave that point along the same tangent line, with slope

10:13.892 --> 10:17.302
four point five, because the slope at zero is the mean.

10:17.400 --> 10:20.932
They differ in curvature. The second derivative at zero is E

10:21.032 --> 10:25.170
of Y squared: twenty-four point seven five for the Poisson, twenty-two

10:25.270 --> 10:28.984
point seven two five for the binomial. The Poisson bends upward

10:29.084 --> 10:32.616
faster, because its variance is larger. Slope at zero is the

10:32.716 --> 10:35.220
mean; curvature at zero carries the spread.

10:37.000 --> 10:41.089
Computing moments is useful, but it is not the main reason

10:41.189 --> 10:45.062
we study MGFs. The main reason is uniqueness. If an MGF

10:45.162 --> 10:48.819
exists, it is unique to its distribution. If Y and Z

10:48.919 --> 10:52.720
have the same MGF on some interval around zero, then Y

10:52.820 --> 10:56.909
and Z have the same distribution. An MGF is a fingerprint.

10:57.000 --> 11:02.299
Example three point twenty-five, in a banking setting. A bank's monthly count of

11:02.399 --> 11:06.618
operational-loss events has m of t equal to e to the three point

11:06.718 --> 11:10.397
two times e to the t minus one. We recognise that: it is

11:10.497 --> 11:14.784
the Poisson MGF with lambda three point two. So Y is Poisson with

11:14.884 --> 11:19.238
mean and variance three point two, and a month with no loss events

11:19.338 --> 11:23.962
has probability e to the minus three point two, about point zero four.

11:25.733 --> 11:30.529
Here are the three MGFs to recognise on sight. Binomial: p e to the

11:30.629 --> 11:34.329
t plus q, to the n. Geometric: p e to the t over one

11:34.429 --> 11:39.810
minus q e to the t, which is exercise three point one forty-seven. Poisson:

11:39.910 --> 11:42.952
e to the lambda times e to the t minus one.

11:43.033 --> 11:48.218
For example, a bank receives twelve loan applications from small businesses, and Y,

11:48.318 --> 11:51.848
the number approved, has m of t equal to point three e to

11:51.948 --> 11:55.987
the t plus point seven, to the twelfth. That is the binomial with

11:56.087 --> 11:59.744
n twelve and p point three. So the mean is three point six,

11:59.844 --> 12:03.755
the variance is two point five two, and the chance that none is

12:03.855 --> 12:07.958
approved is point seven to the twelfth, about point zero one four.

12:09.733 --> 12:15.043
In class this was the think, pair, share exercise. A mobile operator's count of

12:15.143 --> 12:19.769
regional network outages per day has m of t equal to point five, plus

12:19.869 --> 12:23.468
point three e to the t, plus point two e to the two t.

12:23.568 --> 12:28.673
First, what is the distribution of Y? Second, find the mean and the variance

12:28.773 --> 12:32.919
from m of t. Third, check both directly from p of y. Pause the

12:33.019 --> 12:36.617
video now and try it, then come back for the solution.

12:39.700 --> 12:44.554
Here is the solution. Match the MGF term by term with the sum of e

12:44.654 --> 12:48.609
to the t y times p of y. The constant is e to the zero

12:48.709 --> 12:53.038
t, so p of zero is point five. Then p of one is point three

12:53.138 --> 12:58.068
and p of two is point two. By uniqueness, that is the distribution.

12:58.166 --> 13:02.262
m prime of t is point three e to the t plus point four e

13:02.362 --> 13:07.132
to the two t, so the mean is point seven. m double prime is point

13:07.232 --> 13:11.178
three e to the t plus point eight e to the two t, so E

13:11.278 --> 13:16.573
of Y squared is one point one. The variance is one point one minus point

13:16.673 --> 13:22.042
four nine, which is point six one. Directly from p of y: zero times point

13:22.142 --> 13:28.036
five, plus one times point three, plus two times point two, is point seven. Same

13:28.136 --> 13:32.757
numbers. The MGF is just a disciplined way of doing those sums.

13:34.533 --> 13:38.218
Moments are not only a classroom device. Take ten years

13:38.318 --> 13:40.971
of daily log returns on the S and P five

13:41.071 --> 13:46.202
hundred, from FRED, September twenty sixteen to September twenty twenty-six:

13:46.302 --> 13:51.020
two thousand five hundred and thirteen trading days. Standardise them,

13:51.120 --> 13:53.842
so that Z has mean zero and variance one.

13:53.933 --> 13:58.336
A normal distribution has third central moment zero and fourth moment three.

13:58.436 --> 14:01.714
The data give minus point six eight for the third, a left

14:01.814 --> 14:05.980
tail, and nineteen point seven for the fourth. Not three: nearly twenty.

14:06.066 --> 14:10.717
And a day beyond three standard deviations happened on one point four

14:10.817 --> 14:15.468
percent of days, against point two seven percent for the normal: more

14:15.568 --> 14:19.806
than five times as often. The worst day, the sixteenth of March

14:19.906 --> 14:25.383
twenty twenty, was eleven standard deviations below the mean. The histogram rises

14:25.483 --> 14:29.238
above the normal curve in the middle and in the tails. A

14:29.338 --> 14:34.333
model with normal moments understates both the quiet days and the crashes.

14:34.433 --> 14:37.156
The fourth moment is how we measure that.

14:38.933 --> 14:42.879
First. Daily chargebacks at a card acquirer have m of t equal to

14:42.979 --> 14:46.103
e to the two point five times e to the t minus one.

14:46.203 --> 14:49.517
What is the variance of Y? Pause if you want to think.

14:52.100 --> 14:55.320
The answer is two point five. That is the Poisson MGF with

14:55.420 --> 14:58.870
lambda two point five, and a Poisson variance equals its mean.

14:58.966 --> 15:03.537
Second. Out of fifteen export shipments, Y are held at customs, and

15:03.637 --> 15:06.395
m of t is point two e to the t plus point

15:06.495 --> 15:10.229
eight, to the fifteenth. What is the distribution of Y?

15:12.800 --> 15:17.672
Binomial with n fifteen and p point two. Careful: p is the number

15:17.772 --> 15:22.339
multiplying e to the t, not the other one. The mean is three.

15:22.433 --> 15:26.003
Third. A fee schedule charges W equals two Y

15:26.103 --> 15:29.255
plus three manat. What is the MGF of W?

15:31.833 --> 15:34.688
It is e to the three t times m Y of two t.

15:34.788 --> 15:38.838
The shift comes out as a factor, and the scale goes inside.

15:40.633 --> 15:45.577
To sum up. Mean and variance do not determine a distribution; the full

15:45.677 --> 15:52.205
sequence of moments usually does. The moment-generating function packs every moment into one

15:52.305 --> 15:56.889
function. Differentiate k times and set t to zero to read off the

15:56.989 --> 16:01.212
k-th moment. A linear change, a Y plus b, turns the MGF into

16:01.312 --> 16:04.094
e to the b t times m Y of a t. And above

16:04.194 --> 16:08.850
all, uniqueness: recognise the MGF, and you know the distribution.

16:10.633 --> 16:14.105
For practice, do the exercises at the end of section three

16:14.205 --> 16:18.294
point nine: start with three point one forty-five and one forty-six,

16:18.394 --> 16:22.359
then one forty-nine and one fifty-three to one fifty-five, and one

16:22.459 --> 16:26.794
fifty-six and one fifty-seven for the linear change. Week eight, problem

16:26.894 --> 16:29.936
set one is open on WeBWorK and closes on Sunday the

16:30.036 --> 16:34.001
eighth of November at eleven fifty-nine at night. On Saturday, the

16:34.101 --> 16:40.223
thirty-first of October, we continue with probability-generating functions and Tchebysheff's theorem.

16:40.323 --> 16:43.734
If anything here is unclear, ask in our WhatsApp group or

16:43.834 --> 16:46.321
come to office hours. See you on Saturday.
